   247	**2** **CROSS-LANGUAGE ANALYSIS CHALLENGES**
   248	
   249	
   250	This section discusses challenges that arise when implementing
   251	cross-language analyses by integrating single-language analyses. In
   252	total, we identified three challenges that we illustrate at the examples of Java-JavaScript and Java-Native cross-language interactions.
   253	Challenge 1 and 3 describe general problems and also apply to other
   254	cross-language analysis approaches.
   255	**Challenge 1** : **Detecting Cross-language Interaction**
   256	Cross-language analyses first must detect cross-language interactions. Depending on the languages, this is more or less difficult.
   257	Listing 1 illustrates a Java-JavaScript cross-language interaction,
   258	where Lines 5-6 setup a ScriptEngine JavaScript interpreter, Line
   259	7 sets the value of JavaScript variables x and y, Line 9 evaluates
   260	a piece of JavaScript code, and Line 10 retrieves the value of the
   261	JavaScript variable result . The detection of this cross-language
   262	interaction is difficult because the calls to the JavaScript interpreter
   263	are not easily distinguishable from regular intra-language calls and
   264	could even be located in different Java methods. Furthermore, the
   265	JavaScript code itself is dynamically generated while the Java program is running (Line 9). Reliably detecting such cross-language
   266	interaction requires precise Java points-to and string analysis information.
   267	
   268	**Challenge 2** : **Translating Between Analysis Lattices**
   269	Analyses for different languages represent their information with
   270	different lattices. For example, OPAL’s Java points-to analysis represents pointer information with sets of allocation-sites of objects
   271	P(JavaAllocSite) and TAJS’ JavaScript analysis operates on a reduced product of approximations of each JavaScript type Undef ×
   272	Null × Bool × Num × String × P(JavaScriptAllocSite) [ 8 ]. Hence, a
   273	cross-language analysis needs to translate from the lattice of one
   274	analysis to the lattice of another analysis. This translation can be
   275	difficult, because one lattice may contain more or less information
   276	than the other lattice. For example, OPAL’s Java lattice does not
