     1	# AXA: Cross-Language Analysis through Integration of Single-Language Analyses
     2	
     3	This is the artifact of the paper:
     4	_AXA: Cross-Language Analysis through Integration of Single-Language Analyses_
     5	
     6	
     7	## Prerequisites
     8	- Docker must be installed
     9	- At least 16 GB RAM
    10	- Create the docker group:   
    11	  `sudo groupadd docker`
    12	- Add the current user to the docker group:  
    13	  `sudo usermod -aG docker $USER`
    14	
    15	### Creating the container
    16	`./createContainer.sh`    
    17	This command creates the docker container, downloading all necessary dependencies in the process.
    18	
    19	### Starting the Container
    20	`docker run -it axaimage` 
    21	This command starts the docker container and opens a terminal in which all commands listed below can be executed.
    22	
    23	## Starting the Evaluation
    24	All scripts to reproduce the evaluation results (Table I, Table II, and Table III) are located in /runner. 
    25	
    26	### Table I (Implementation Effort)
    27	To get the lines of code (Table I) of the extensions and changes of the single-language analyses for the integration into AXA run the following command:
    28	```
    29	/runner/printLOCs.sh
    30	```
    31	
    32	### Table II (Benchmark)
    33	The evaluation of Java-JS testcases and Java-Native testcases is performed separately.
    34	To run the evaluation of annotated test cases (Table II), execute the scripts `runJSBenchmark.sh` (JavaScript testcases) and `runNativeBenchmark.sh` (native testcases).
    35	This will run a cross-language points-to-analysis on the annotated benchmarks. 
    36	Each testcase is annotated with expected points-to sets of references affected by cross-language interactions.
    37	A testcase is considered as passed iff the points-to analysis results match the annotation.
    38	
    39	To evaluate Java-JavaScript testcases:
    40	```
    41	/runner/runJSBenchmark.sh
    42	```
    43	
    44	To evaluate Java-Native testcases:
    45	```
    46	/runner/runNativeBenchmark.sh
    47	```
    48	
    49	### Table III (Precision & Recall of AXA vs. single language)
    50	To measure precision and recall of points-to analysis (Table III), execute the scripts `precisionRecallJS.sh` (JavaScript testcases) and `precisionRecallNative` (native testcases).
    51	This experiment first performs an instrumentation on the benchmark cases to log instance-ids of reference variables at all possible points in code.
    52	The benchmarks are executed and instance ids are logged to a file.
    53	Then, the benchmark code is analyzed first with a Java-only points-to analysis, then with our cross-language points-to analysis.
    54	The points-to sets of _all_ references in code are matched with instances logged by the instrumentation to calculate precision and recall.
    55	
    56	To calculate precision/recall of points-to analysis on Java-JavaScript testcases:
    57	```
    58	/runner/precisionRecallJS.sh
    59	```
    60	
    61	To calculate precision/recall of points-to analysis on Java-Native testcases:
    62	```
    63	/runner/precisionRecallNative.sh
    64	```
    65	
    66	
    67	## Reusability
    68	
    69	To analyse arbitrary Java-Native-code using AXA, launch the `org.opalj.xl.Coordinator` class. The current implementation will perform a cross-language points-to analysis and output an overview of detected cross-language interactions.
    70	
    71	Specify java classes to be analyzed using the `-cp` argument.
